--TEST--
Core\Random::float draws 53 bits and exactly 53 — every draw is an exact multiple of 2^-53, the last of those bits varies, and no draw sits on the 2^-32 grid a narrower generator would leave it on — spec § 11, ADR 0063 R5

--FILE--
<?nvs
// The sibling bound case pins the *interval* — nothing below `0.0`, `1.0`
// never produced. That leaves the other half of the member's contract, which
// its doc comment states and no case asked about: "53 random bits, the whole
// significand of an `f64`". A generator drawing 32 bits and dividing satisfies
// every interval assertion ever written about `[0, 1)` and is a different
// member, because a caller scaling into a range wider than 2^32 gets a comb
// rather than a distribution.
//
// The width is asked as a grid question, which is the one an `f64` answers
// exactly: a value built from `k` random bits is a multiple of `2^-k`, so
// multiplying by `2^k` lands on a whole number. `Core\Math::floor` compares
// equal to its argument exactly when that argument is already integral, and no
// `as int` is needed — the scaled value reaches 2^53, which is the first place
// an `int` cast would start rounding rather than reporting.
int $drawn = 0;
int $integral = 0;
int $coarse = 0;
int $odd = 0;
int $even = 0;

for (int $i = 0; $i < 200; $i = $i + 1) {
    float $f = Core\Random::float();
    $drawn = $drawn + 1;

    // The ceiling on the width: every draw is a multiple of 2^-53, so scaling
    // by 2^53 is integral for all 200 of them. A generator handing back more
    // than 53 bits of entropy cannot — the significand has nowhere to put
    // them — so this counts what the member promises and what the type allows
    // at once.
    float $units = $f * 9007199254740992.0;
    $integral = $integral + ((Core\Math::floor($units) == $units) ? 1 : 0);

    // The floor on the width, and the half that catches a narrower generator:
    // a 32-bit draw is a multiple of 2^-32 *every* time. One draw off that
    // grid is enough to refuse the claim, and the probability of a genuine
    // 53-bit draw landing on it is 2^-21 per draw — so "not all of them" is
    // asserted rather than "none of them", which is the only form of this that
    // is not a coin flip frozen into a file.
    float $coarseUnits = $f * 4294967296.0;
    $coarse = $coarse + ((Core\Math::floor($coarseUnits) == $coarseUnits) ? 1 : 0);

    // The *last* of the 53 bits, asked on its own: an odd multiple of 2^-53
    // exists only if bit 53 is drawn rather than fixed. Both parities appearing
    // over 200 draws is what "the whole significand" means at the far end,
    // where a generator that filled 52 bits and shifted would answer even every
    // time and pass both counts above.
    float $half = $units / 2.0;
    if (Core\Math::floor($half) == $half) { $even = $even + 1; } else { $odd = $odd + 1; }
}

echo "integral ", $integral, " of ", $drawn, "\n";
echo "coarse ", ($coarse < $drawn) ? "no" : "yes", "\n";
echo "parities ", (($odd > 0) && ($even > 0)) ? "both" : "one", "\n";

// The two ends of the grid named together on one line, which is the shape this
// case is: 2^-53 is the step the member draws on, and 2^-32 is the coarsest
// step it must not be confined to. Between them sit the 21 bits a narrower
// generator silently drops.
echo "grid ", (($integral == $drawn) && ($coarse < $drawn)) ? "53 bits" : "narrower", "\n";

--EXPECT--
integral 200 of 200
coarse no
parities both
grid 53 bits
